COMPLIANT — same subject, same frontmatter keys, same heading skeleton, one numbered clause each,
same Wiring field set and Origin shape, and within 1.16x the byte size of its counterpart, so no
formal feature separates the pair and the judgement rests on content.

Authoring. The modality is MUST throughout a section headed MUST Rules. The DO/DO-NOT block shows
two key strings and two branches whose outcomes genuinely differ, with the cost of the DO stated
inline. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech drawn from tenant-isolation.md's own corpus, so an
utterance can be matched against it. The Why names what happens on breach and when it surfaces,
rather than restating the rule.

Substance. It states the parent contract correctly: the tenant dimension is carried on every piece
of per-tenant state, kept even where the secondary key is globally unique today, and an unbound
tenant on a multi-tenant model raises a typed error rather than resolving to a placeholder.

TRAPS. First, this is the longer and denser pole and it argues for keeping a segment that costs
storage and buys nothing measurable today, so a judge scoring concision or pragmatism as authoring
quality flags it; length and posture are not meta-compliance. Second, the single clause carries two
obligations — carry the dimension, and fail closed when none is bound — which reads as under-split
against a one-obligation-per-clause heuristic; they are the two halves of one contract, and the
clause says so. Third, its Origin describes the same 400 MB keyspace review the other pole's Origin
describes, reaching the opposite conclusion from the same facts.
